.TH HIDBOOT 2
.SH NAME
hidboot \- the HID boot protocol
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "hidboot.m";
hidboot := load Hidboot Hidboot->PATH;

init:    fn();
keysym:  fn(k, mod: int): string;
keysof:  fn(rep: array of byte): array of int;
held:    fn(keys: array of int, k: int): int;
mouse:   fn(rep: array of byte): (int, int, int);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Hidboot
reads the HID boot protocol: the small fixed subset of HID that a
machine's own firmware drives before any driver is loaded.  Its
keyboard report is eight bytes and its mouse report is three, with a
layout that is the same on every device that claims to speak it, so
nothing has to be read off the device \- which is the whole reason the
boot protocol exists.
.PP
.B Init
must be called before anything else.
.PP
.BI keysof( rep )
is the key codes in an eight byte keyboard report, in order, with the
empty slots and the rollover code a keyboard sends when too many keys
are down left out.
.PP
A boot report is the state of the keyboard and not an event: the keys
that are down, in no particular order.  A key that is held appears in
every report until it is let go, so a caller keeps the previous
report and types only what was not down before.
.BI held( keys ,\  k )
is what asks that question.
.PP
.BI keysym( k ,\  mod )
is what key code
.I k
means with modifier byte
.I mod
\- the text to write to
.B /dev/keyboard
\- or nil for a key this does not map: caps lock, the function keys
and the arrows, which need more than a character each.  The table is
the US layout, which is what the boot protocol assumes: a device that
speaks it reports positions, and what a position means is the host's
business.
.PP
.BI mouse( rep )
is a three byte mouse report: the movement, and then the buttons in
the order
.B /dev/pointer
wants them, which is not the order HID reports them in.  The movement
is a change and not a position, so a caller adds it up.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/hidboot.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR hidreport (2),
.IR btfs (4),
.IR usbd (1)
.SH BUGS
The keyboard table is the US layout and nothing selects another.
